{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And behold, there came up out of the river</b> — because the land of Egypt drinks from the river, and from it comes to them both satiety and famine, he saw them coming up from the river. And the cows are a sign for plowing, and the ears of grain are a sign for harvest, as it says (Genesis 45:6), \"where there is neither plowing nor harvest.\" He saw that the river had risen only a little, and there was no plowing, and that what they would sow in the moist places, an east wind, the wind of YHWH, would come and burn them up, as he saw them blasted by the east wind. And what appears from the implication of the verses is that the satiety was in the land of Egypt alone, as it says (Genesis 41:29), \"great satiety throughout all the land of Egypt,\" and likewise (Genesis 41:48), \"which were in the land of Egypt\"; but the famine was in all the lands (Genesis 41:54). And so Joseph interpreted it: \"and there shall arise seven years of famine,\" and he did not mention \"in the land of Egypt\"; therefore they could not gather food in the other lands, even if they heard the matter, for it was very widely publicized. And perhaps there was in the dream a hint of this in that it mentions \"and they grazed in the reed grass,\" for there was their pasture and their standing-place, but the lean cows, after eating the good ones, would wander through the land, and Pharaoh did not know where they went." "<b>In the reed grass</b> — a marsh, like \"will the reed grass grow\" (Job 8:11); this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 41:2). But this is not correct, for אָחוּ is the name of the growing grass, as in \"Can the papyrus grow up without a marsh? Will the reed grass flourish without water? While it is yet in its freshness and not plucked\" (Job 8:11–12) — and it is not the marsh itself. Perhaps he means to say that the grass that grows in marshes is called by the name of the marsh. But the correct interpretation is that אָחוּ is a comprehensive term for the meadow-grass and vegetation that grows on the banks of rivers and marshes, and the bet in בָּאָחוּ is like the bet in \"eat of my bread and drink of the wine I have mingled\" (Proverbs 9:5), for they were grazing on the bank of the river, as it says (Genesis 41:3), \"beside the cows on the bank of the river.\" Or perhaps it is from the root אַחְוָה, because many species of grass grow there together."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The phrase רוח ה׳ (wind of YHWH) appears in the Hebrew; rendered with the divine name per protocol."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:36.015746525Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}